# Red t’ blue documentary

- Focusing on how, and why, the ex-mining town constituency of Mansfield swung from the Labour party, which had held it for a consecutive 94-years, to the Conservative party in the 2017 snap-election.
- Alan Meale was Labour MP for Mansfield 1987-2017
    - ‘Went on for too long’
- Sid Pepper - UKIP candidate for Mansfield
    - ‘Strong movement against immigration’
- Mansfield had already faced economic deprivation due to Thatcher
- Brexit
    - Seen as counterculture
    - Leave means leave → 70.9% one of the largest votes to leave in the whole country
    - Can’t see what EU has done for Mansfield cause of the deprivation
- Alan Meale was too old fashioned and was replaced by Ben Bradley
- Ben Bradley was a campaign manager turned politician
    - Took 9,000 of the UKIP vote
    - UKIP led votes from Labour to the Conservatives
- Won by a 16,306 majority in 2019
- Many ex-coalfield towns followed suit, raising the Blue Banner where a Red one had flown for so long
- Lee Anderson MP and ex-Labour supporter
    - Grew up on Labour but experienced partisan dealignment
    - First MP in Ashfield to have come from there
- ‘If you recognise and want to change it you join the Labour party, Tories don’t have that philosophy’
    - Progressive party
- Headstocks of the mines - some want restored, some want them removed
